{"authored" [{"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$\n\nInterpret the printed inequalities literally. If they define a degenerate set, use the zero-area surface-integral convention. What is the flux?\n\nHint: First check whether $-x\\le y\\le0$ can have a nonempty interval when $x\\le0$. Treat the printed inequalities literally." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/review.json" "file_sha256" "c85c0d817c4201c07ba6301025164447542e02394ff8301471d8f3984ee95cf8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "1c422d8b2ebc3f8353fc1f326eab4b4a201cca0a45741e5d4c56d527b7cd7140" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/verification.json" "basis" 2265 "rationale" "Retain original inequalities, graph and field literally. They force projection{(0,0)}, area0 and flux0 under the zero-area convention; graph is degenerate, not regular2D. Source accepted b is only the formal reversed-bound integral. New field version appends0 and retains original fields/answers/history without inferring replacement bounds."} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The original inequalities are retained literally. Since $-\\pi\\le x\\le0$, we have $-x\\ge0$. The additional inequalities $-x\\le y\\le0$ are possible only when $-x=0$, so $x=0$ and $y=0$. Therefore the projection is $D=\\{(0,0)\\}$ and its two-dimensional area is zero. The graph $z=\\cos(x+y)$ over $D$ consists only of $(0,0,1)$; it is a degenerate surface, not a regular two-dimensional patch.\n\nUnder the zero-area surface-integral convention, both its surface area and its flux are zero:\n\n$$\\operatorname{area}(S)=0,\\qquad\\iint_S\\langle1,0,y\\rangle\\cdot d\\mathbf S=0.$$\n\nOne may extend the upward graph vector area element from the surrounding smooth graph: $(-g_x,-g_y,1)=(\\sin(x+y),\\sin(x+y),1)$. Its dot product with $F=\\langle1,0,y\\rangle$ is $\\sin(x+y)+y$. Integrating this bounded continuous function over the literal zero-area set $D$ yields zero.\n\nThe source accepted choice b, $\\pi-\\pi^3/6$, and its revealed solution instead evaluated the formal signed repeated integral\n\n$$\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\int_{-x}^{0}(\\sin(x+y)+y)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_{-\\pi}^{0}\\left(1-\\cos x-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)\\,dx=\\pi-\\frac{\\pi^3}{6}.$$\n\nFor $x<0$, these inner limits are reversed. A formal signed integral over reversed limits does not integrate over the region described by the original inequalities. No corrected bounds are inferred. The study answer is $0$ for the original literal inequalities; the source's choice, grading and calculation remain preserved as evidence." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/review.json" "file_sha256" "c85c0d817c4201c07ba6301025164447542e02394ff8301471d8f3984ee95cf8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "1c422d8b2ebc3f8353fc1f326eab4b4a201cca0a45741e5d4c56d527b7cd7140" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/verification.json" "basis" 2265 "rationale" "Retain original inequalities, graph and field literally. They force projection{(0,0)}, area0 and flux0 under the zero-area convention; graph is degenerate, not regular2D. Source accepted b is only the formal reversed-bound integral. New field version appends0 and retains original fields/answers/history without inferring replacement bounds."} {"question" "q-161481" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/review.json" "file_sha256" "c85c0d817c4201c07ba6301025164447542e02394ff8301471d8f3984ee95cf8" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "1c422d8b2ebc3f8353fc1f326eab4b4a201cca0a45741e5d4c56d527b7cd7140" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161481/verification.json" "basis" 2265 "rationale" "Retain original inequalities, graph and field literally. They force projection{(0,0)}, area0 and flux0 under the zero-area convention; graph is degenerate, not regular2D. Source accepted b is only the formal reversed-bound integral. New field version appends0 and retains original fields/answers/history without inferring replacement bounds."} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ for $-\\pi \\le x\\le 0$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩{|}_{z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)} \\\\  & =⟨{{1}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨1,\\,0,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))-0\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y))+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+0)-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-0+(-\\pi )-\\frac{(-\\pi )^{3}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/state.json" "file_sha256" "b65937d72afce5260534d53d0c48a0a2dadbce7b5a63ad0dc73a4e54242f6b9c" "basis" 2263 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067725/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161481" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "c